# The Oregon lottery: what randomization actually buys you
next: {"title":"The RAND Health Insurance Experiment: randomizing the price of care","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/the-rand-health-insurance-experiment-randomizing-the-price-of-care.cast"}

## Independence: (Y(1),Y(0)) ⊥ D
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_steps
with:
    title: What the lottery buys
    steps:
      - {tex: 'Y_i = D_i\,Y_i(1) + (1-D_i)\,Y_i(0)', note: We see one outcome per person.}
      - {tex: 'E[Y_i\mid D_i=1]-E[Y_i\mid D_i=0]=\underbrace{E[Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)\mid D_i=1]}_{\text{ATT}}+\underbrace{E[Y_i(0)\mid D_i=1]-E[Y_i(0)\mid D_i=0]}_{\text{selection bias}}', note: Lecture 2's decomposition.}
      - {tex: '\bigl(Y_i(1),\,Y_i(0)\bigr)\ \perp\ D_i', note: What the draw delivers.}
      - {tex: 'E[Y_i\mid D_i=1]-E[Y_i\mid D_i=0]=E[Y_i(1)]-E[Y_i(0)]', note: Selection bias = 0. This is the ATE.}

    mark mark_ate target step_3 kind box hidden true
    box n_list "≈90,000 signed up" hidden true
    chance n_draw "Lottery" hidden true
    box n_treat "≈30,000 drawn: D=1" #2f6b8f hidden true
    box n_ctrl "≈60,000 not drawn: D=0" #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a1 n_list -> n_draw hidden true
    arrow a2 n_draw -> n_treat curved #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a3 n_draw -> n_ctrl curved #b5482e hidden true

Now that we have seen Oregon draw thirty thousand names, the question is what that draw licenses you to claim — because with any other data, comparing the insured to the uninsured is a mistake, and here it is not.
    draw title step_0 note_0

    pause 0.4

From Lecture 2: the raw difference in means is the effect on the treated, plus a term comparing the two groups' untreated outcome Y_i(0).
    draw step_1 note_1

That second bracket is the whole problem — the people who get insured would have had different health anyway, and no arithmetic on observed data pulls it apart from the effect.
    highlight step_1 effect glow

The lottery's entire contribution is this line: a machine that knows nothing about you assigns D, so treatment is independent of both potential outcomes.
    draw step_2 note_2

    pause 0.3

Far stronger than equal average age — the full joint distribution of Y(1) and Y(0) matches across arms, motivation and family support and every trait nobody wrote down included.
    highlight step_2 effect glow

So the untreated outcome has the same expectation in both arms, the bias term is exactly zero, and a difference of raw averages is the average causal effect.
    draw step_3 note_3

No covariates, no functional form: the design is doing the identifying, and the regression is just bookkeeping.
    draw mark_ate delivery grave

    quiz "In the decomposition, what does independence set to zero?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Independence makes the untreated outcome equal in expectation across arms, so the selection-bias term vanishes.
        wrong: The causal effect survives; it is the comparison of untreated outcomes across the two groups that disappears.
        * The treatment effect itself
        + The selection-bias term
        * The variance of Y

Now, who exactly does that promise cover?
    clear

Roughly ninety thousand low-income uninsured adults signed onto Oregon's waiting list within five weeks.
    draw n_list

The state had money for about ten thousand slots, and rationed them by drawing names.
    draw n_draw a1

Thirty thousand won the chance to apply; the sixty thousand left behind became the control group.
    draw n_treat n_ctrl a2 a3 parallel true

Since the draw ignored everything about them, both arms carry the same distribution of potential outcomes — including the traits you could never put in a regression.
    label lab_indep "Same distribution of Y(1), Y(0) in both arms" below attach_to n_draw

And Oregon never intended an experiment: the lottery was simply the fairest way to ration scarce slots, and economists recognised a randomized trial in a bureaucratic accident.
    highlight n_draw effect circle

    quiz "Why can't matching on observables promise the same thing?" required true
        right: Matching balances the covariates in your dataset; the lottery balances everything, measured or not.
        wrong: Sample size is not the issue — matching can only balance the variables you actually observed.
        * It needs a larger sample
        + It balances only what you measured
        * It cannot handle binary treatment

Randomization never makes two people alike; it makes assignment independent of who they are — and that alone turns a difference in averages into a causal effect.

## Reading a Balance Table
voice: female
level: advanced

    text a_title "Oregon lottery: pre-randomization characteristics" x 500 y 690 font_size 30 hidden true
    text a_h1 "Characteristic" #8f887c x 230 y 620 font_size 25 hidden true
