    text b_r1c1 "Female" x 230 y 540 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r1c2 "0.56" x 520 y 540 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r1c3 "0.55" x 690 y 540 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r1c4 "+0.01" x 855 y 540 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r2c1 "Age (years)" x 230 y 490 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r2c2 "38.4" x 520 y 490 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r2c3 "42.9" x 690 y 490 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r2c4 "−4.5 ***" #b5482e x 855 y 490 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r3c1 "Prior hospital stay" x 230 y 440 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r3c2 "0.041" x 520 y 440 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r3c3 "0.089" x 690 y 440 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r3c4 "−0.048 ***" #b5482e x 855 y 440 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r4c1 "Signed up on day 1" x 230 y 390 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r4c2 "0.71" x 520 y 390 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r4c3 "0.52" x 690 y 390 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_r4c4 "+0.19 ***" #b5482e x 855 y 390 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b_ftest "Joint F-test: p < 0.001 — balance rejected" #b5482e x 500 y 315 font_size 26 hidden true
    mark b_mark #b5482e target b_ftest kind box hidden true
    text c_1 "A balance table cannot test independence." x 500 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c_2 "It tests whether the lottery was run as claimed." #2f6b8f x 500 y 445 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark c_mark #2f6b8f target c_2 kind box hidden true

Now that we have seen what independence promises, here is the catch: it is a claim about potential outcomes we never observe, so nobody can test it directly.
    draw a_title

So we test its observable shadow — characteristics fixed before the draw, which winning a lottery could not possibly have changed.
    draw a_h1 a_h2 a_h3 a_h4 a_rule parallel true

Oregon's own audit, across the roughly seventy-five thousand people in the study: about fifty-six percent women on each side, and a mean age near forty-one on each side.
    draw a_r1c1 a_r1c2 a_r1c3 a_r1c4 a_r2c1 a_r2c2 a_r2c3 a_r2c4 parallel true

    pause 0.4

Language from the sign-up form, and hospital stays pulled from state discharge records before the draw — a measure nobody could report to their own advantage.
    draw a_r3c1 a_r3c2 a_r3c3 a_r3c4 a_r4c1 a_r4c2 a_r4c3 a_r4c4 parallel true

Read down the last column: the gaps are fractions of a percentage point, and they do not all lean the same way.
    focus a_r1c4 a_r2c4 a_r3c4 a_r4c4

The decisive test is joint — put every covariate together and ask whether they predict who was drawn; in Oregon, they cannot.
    draw a_ftest a_mark

    quiz "In a lottery that truly worked, what should twenty separate balance comparisons at the 5% level show?" intro "Quick check."
        right: About one in twenty looks significant by pure chance — which is why the joint test, not a single starred row, decides.
        wrong: Randomization removes systematic gaps, not sampling noise.
        * All twenty balanced
        + About one significant by chance
        * Any starred row voids the study

One covariate is imbalanced on purpose: the lottery drew individuals but covered everyone on their household application, so larger households were likelier to win — which is why every Oregon regression controls for household size.
    draw a_hh

    clear

Now the counterexample — a table from a lottery that did not work.
    draw b_title b_h1 b_h2 b_h3 b_h4 b_rule

The first row looks fine, which proves nothing; one balanced characteristic is easy to come by.
    draw b_r1c1 b_r1c2 b_r1c3 b_r1c4 parallel true

But the winners are four and a half years younger, half as likely to have been hospitalized before the draw, and far likelier to have signed up on day one.
    draw b_r2c1 b_r2c2 b_r2c3 b_r2c4 b_r3c1 b_r3c2 b_r3c3 b_r3c4 b_r4c1 b_r4c2 b_r4c3 b_r4c4 parallel true

They all point the same way — toward a healthier, keener group — and each is many times its standard error; that is a pattern, not noise.
    highlight b_r2c4 b_r3c4 b_r4c4 effect glow color #b5482e

The joint test rejects at p below one in a thousand: whatever sorted these two groups, it was not a coin.
    draw b_ftest b_mark delivery grave

    quiz "A balance table fails like this. What is the most likely explanation?" intro "One more."
        right: Systematic gaps usually mean the analysed sample was formed after randomization — differential attrition, or a subgroup defined by post-draw behaviour.
        wrong: Balance is about how the two groups were formed, not about the size of any effect.
        * The treatment effect is zero
        + Something shaped the groups after the draw
        * Randomization simply failed

    clear

So the audit is narrower than it looks: it never verifies the assumption about unobservables, it only checks that the machinery behaved.
    draw c_1 c_2

That check mattered here, because Oregon in 2008 was the first randomized test of extending Medicaid coverage in about four decades — one shot, and everything downstream rests on it.
    text c_3 "Oregon, 2008 — the first randomized test of extending" #8f887c x 500 y 330 font_size 24
    text c_4 "Medicaid coverage in roughly four decades." #8f887c x 500 y 295 font_size 24

A balance table cannot prove that winners and losers had the same potential outcomes; it can only fail to catch a lottery that broke — and the joint test is what decides.
    draw c_mark delivery grave

## What a Lottery Can and Cannot Settle
voice: female
level: advanced
use: icon_array
with:
    total: 100
    groups:
      - {label: Covered by the win, count: 25, color: supply}
    rest_label: Won, never covered
    title: 100 lottery winners

Now that we have the two-year results in hand, we can ask precisely what that lottery settled — and what no lottery could.
    draw title

Here are a hundred of the roughly thirty thousand people whose names Oregon drew from the ninety thousand who signed up.
    draw rest

    pause 0.3

Winning raised the chance of actually being on Medicaid by about twenty-five percentage points — one in four. The rest never enrolled: paperwork unreturned, income too high, letters gone astray.
    draw group_0 legend_0 legend_rest

The draw was independent of potential outcomes, so winners and losers are exchangeable — but the draw is not coverage, and D, actually holding insurance, still depended on who followed through.
    highlight rest effect glow

    quiz "So what does the raw winners-minus-losers difference estimate?"
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: It is the intention-to-treat effect — the effect of the offer, diluted by the three in four who never got covered.
        wrong: It cannot be an effect of coverage: three quarters of these winners were never covered at all.
        * The effect of coverage on everyone
        + The effect of being offered coverage
        * The effect of coverage on those who enrolled

Dividing that offer effect by the quarter turns it into an effect on compliers — and multiplies the confidence interval by four along with it.
    highlight group_0 effect glow

    pause 0.5

Coverage moved plenty: about a third more outpatient visits, catastrophic out-of-pocket spending down four and a half points, depression screening down nine points — but on blood pressure, cholesterol and blood sugar, nothing.
    clear

That silence deserves a magnifying glass, so put one outcome on a line, with zero meaning nothing moved.
    text null_title "Coverage and blood pressure, two years" x 500 y 610 font_size 30
    path effect_axis #8f887c points [[180,330],[860,330]]
    text axis_caption "Change in systolic blood pressure (mm Hg)" #8f887c x 500 y 288 font_size 22
    path zero_line dashed #8f887c points [[560,310],[560,500]]
    text zero_label "0 = no effect" #8f887c x 650 y 518 font_size 22

After two years and roughly twelve thousand in-person examinations, the estimate landed at about minus half a millimetre of mercury — indistinguishable from nothing.
    shape est_point #2f6b8f shape circle x 530 y 420 radius 10 style.fill #2f6b8f
    text est_label "estimate ≈ −0.5" #2f6b8f x 420 y 372 font_size 22

    pause 0.3

But the interval around it runs from roughly minus three to plus two millimetres.
    path ci_bar #2f6b8f points [[380,420],[680,420]] style.stroke_width 6
    path cap_lo #2f6b8f points [[380,398],[380,442]]
    path cap_hi #2f6b8f points [[680,398],[680,442]]
    text label_lo "−3" #2f6b8f x 380 y 468 font_size 22
    text label_hi "+2" #2f6b8f x 680 y 468 font_size 22

    quiz "What does that interval license us to say?"
        right: A null bounds an effect, it does not zero it — a three-point fall still sits comfortably inside this interval.
        wrong: An interval that wide is silence, not proof of zero — it simply rules out large effects.
        * Coverage has no effect on blood pressure
        + Coverage's effect is at most a few mm Hg
        * The experiment was badly run

And that width is the price of one-in-four take-up: scaling the offer up by four scales the uncertainty up by four too — the precision problem this course comes back to.
    mark width_mark target ci_bar kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

Randomization also says nothing about who was in the room: low-income uninsured adults who put their names on an Oregon list in 2008.
    text limit_1 "Who: low-income uninsured adults, Oregon, 2008" x 500 y 205 font_size 24

Nor about time — two years, for conditions that do their damage over decades.
    text limit_2 "How long: two years" x 500 y 160 font_size 24

What it does buy is airtight: within these people and this window, the effect of the offer is measured without a single untestable assumption.
    text limit_3 "What: the effect of the offer, scaled to compliers" x 500 y 115 font_size 24

That is the trade the Oregon lottery makes, and the lesson of this whole series — randomization buys certainty about a narrow question, and everything wider is an argument you still have to make.

